libv4l-rkmpp: let the VPU read the capture buffer in place

Companion to 0001 and 0002. Those made the encoders reachable; this
makes them cheap. Measured against ustreamer 6.65 on an OrangePi RK3399
at 1080p60, with kvmd launching the streamer as it does in production:
ustreamer went from 86% of a core to 9%, and the JPEG stream from 30 fps
to ~45 fps, with byte-identical output.

1. Encoder input dma-bufs were copied, not imported.

   A client that queues a raw frame as V4L2_MEMORY_DMABUF - which is
   what ustreamer does for H.264, and for JPEG in m2m-video mode - gets
   no benefit from it here. rkmpp_copy_buffer() maps the dma-buf and
   memcpy()s it into one of the plugin's own MPP buffers, every frame.
   ustreamer reports "DMA=1" and the copy happens anyway, one layer down.

   That copy is not cheap. The frame comes from the ISP, a non-coherent
   device, so the CPU sees the buffer uncached: copying 4 MB of 1080p
   YUYV out of it measured ~27 ms of a core per frame, and two JPEG
   workers at 30 fps spent ~80% of a core doing nothing else. The VPU
   needs none of it - VEPU2 reads its input by fd through its own IOMMU,
   and MPP's EXT_DMA allocator only maps a buffer if someone asks for a
   CPU pointer, which the encoder path never does.

   Import the dma-buf with mpp_buffer_import() and hand that to the
   encoder instead. The import is held from QBUF until the packet comes
   back, i.e. until the hardware has finished reading, and dropped
   before the buffer is returned to the client.

   The buffer has to cover what the hardware reads, not what the frame
   holds, and those differ: VEPU2 fetches whole macroblock rows, so a
   1080-line frame is read as 1088 lines. Given a buffer of exactly 1080
   the encoder's IOMMU faulted on the first address past the end
   (buffer + 1081 * stride), the VPU hung, and MPP reset it. The plugin's
   own buffers are sized from the macroblock-rounded format, which is
   why the copy never had this problem. So import only a dma-buf at
   least that large and copy anything smaller, as before. Kernel patch
   0014 makes the ISP allocate capture buffers that pass this check;
   without it every frame is copied and nothing is worse than it was.

   "import-dmabuf=0" in the device file turns the import off, for A/B
   comparison.

2. JPEG quality was lost on the way through m2m-video.

   ustreamer only hands over the dma-buf in --encoder=m2m-video mode,
   and that mode has no quality control: it converts --quality into a
   bitrate and sends V4L2_CID_MPEG_VIDEO_BITRATE. The quality factor is
   pinned here (see rkmpp_enc_apply_mjpeg_cfg), so the bitrate did
   nothing and every --quality encoded as 80 - measured, q30 to q95
   gave byte-identical frames. kvmd's quality setting would have stopped
   working without a word.

   ustreamer's conversion is deterministic and one-to-one over 1..100
   (us_m2m_mjpeg_encoder_init in src/ustreamer/m2m.c), so read the
   quality back out of it. Rather than invert it in floating point, run
   it forward for every quality and accept only an exact match; a
   bitrate that matches nothing leaves the quality alone instead of
   guessing. An explicit V4L2_CID_JPEG_COMPRESSION_QUALITY still wins.

   It is resolved when the JPEG configuration is applied, not when the
   control arrives, because ustreamer sets its controls before its
   format, and until the format is set the encoder does not yet know it
   is a JPEG one.

   Verified: m2m-video at q30, q50, q80 and q95 produces the same bytes
   as m2m-image at the same quality, so a static screen still encodes to
   identical frames and --drop-same-frames still drops them.

